<!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"is-style-label-islamic"} -->
<p class="is-style-label-islamic">Islamic position</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ih-intro"} -->
<p class="ih-intro">This page sets out, step by step, how <a href="/gospel-according-to-islam/">The Gospel According to Islam</a> on this website was put together: whose text it is, how that text was arranged, where the Qur'anic passages and the sayings of Thomas come from, how the notes are numbered, and the rule that decided what was kept and what was set aside. Nothing in the gospel was composed in secret, and a reader who wants to check any part of it will find the source named here.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">A practice with a history</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A Muslim gospel is not a new idea. From the first centuries of Islam, Muslim scholars read, quoted and retold the gospels through the Qur'an. In the ninth century al-Qāsim b. Ibrāhīm (d. 860) reworked Matthew's story this way. Medieval Muslim authors cite all four gospels; one survey counts more than 1,270 references across twenty writers. They did not treat the gospels as forbidden books. They treated them as books that contain the Injīl and other things besides, and they used the Qur'an to tell the two apart. This gospel continues that practice with the tools that now exist: a critical Greek text, dated manuscripts, and two centuries of research into who wrote the gospels and when.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The base text: Ahmad Shafaat, 1985</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The words of the gospel are those of Dr. Ahmad Shafaat, <em>The Gospel According to Islam</em> (New York: Vantage Press, 1985). Shafaat set himself a clear task. The Qur'an has ninety-three verses that refer to Jesus, and between them they speak to almost every part of his story: his family, birth and childhood, the nature of his person and his titles, his signs and his message, his rejection by the Jews, his death and exaltation, and the meaning of his work for Jewish history and for the history of the world. Shafaat took that outline and filled it out with material drawn mainly from the New Testament, sometimes changed to agree with the Qur'anic revelation, to form a gospel of about the size of Mark. He then added extensive notes to explain and support the text, with references to the Qur'an and the New Testament throughout, and he kept the results and debates of modern critical research in view as he wrote. His introduction, in which he explains why a new gospel is justified and asks the Christian reader to read it as he would read a book from another church, is given in full on the <a href="/gospel-according-to-islam/introduction/">Introduction</a> page.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Shafaat's book has twenty-seven chapters. On this website they are the twenty-seven sections, in his order, under his titles. His wording was not rewritten. The only changes to his text are corrections of scanning errors in the copy from which it was typed: stray marks, broken references such as "Micah 5:2" and "Mark 11:1-11", and "Bethsaida!" for Bethsaida. In the notes, "Paul" is written "Paul of Tarsus", as on every other page of this website. Every correction of this kind is listed in the <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The arrangement: The Narrative of Jesus, Son of Mary</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Shafaat printed his gospel in chapter and verse, with the notes at the back. The text here follows a second arrangement of the same material, <em>The Narrative of Jesus, Son of Mary</em>, in which the verses run on as paragraphs, the twenty-seven sections are grouped into six chapters, and the notes stand at the foot of the section they belong to. The six chapters are:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Birth and Childhood</strong> (sections 1 to 4): the family of Amran, Zacharias and John; the annunciation and the birth; the promised messenger, Joseph and the creation of Jesus; the presentation in the temple and the visit to Jerusalem.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>John and the Beginning of the Ministry</strong> (sections 5 to 8): John the Baptist; the baptism, the temptation and the return to Nazareth; Capernaum; the leper, the sabbath and the Beatitudes.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>The Ministry in Galilee</strong> (sections 9 to 13): publicans and fasting; the traditions of the elders; the blind man and the Samaritan woman; Jairus's daughter and the death of John; the twelve and their mission.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Preaching, Signs and the Disciples</strong> (sections 14 to 18): the bird of clay and Beelzebub; signs, the rich man and Lazarus; the table from heaven, Caesarea Philippi and the vision; the parables of the leaven and the tares; greatness, the Samaritans and the children.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Jerusalem</strong> (sections 19 to 24): the rich man and the request of James and John; the entry into Jerusalem and the cleansing of the temple; the questions in the temple; the vineyard; the last supper and the Man of perfection; the hour and the promise of the Counsellor.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Arrest, Trial and Aftermath</strong> (sections 25 to 27): Gethsemane, the arrest and Peter's denial; the trial, Barabbas and the road to Emmaus; the empty tomb, the death of Jesus and the reports.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The narrative opens with the Opening of the Qur'an, al-Fātiḥa, and closes with the Qur'an's own statements on the oneness of God: the Throne Verse (2:255), the Beautiful Names (59:22-24) and Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ (112). A gospel written in the light of the Qur'an begins and ends where the Qur'an does, with God alone. Each section page carries a line naming its chapter, the text in paragraphs, its notes, and links to the previous section, the next, and the contents. A visitor can read the whole from <a href="/gospel-according-to-islam/1/">section 1</a> to <a href="/gospel-according-to-islam/27/">section 27</a> without returning to the index.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The Qur'anic passages set in the story</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Shafaat's text paraphrases the Qur'an where the story needs it. The arrangement used here goes a step further and sets the Qur'an's own words into the narrative at the points where they fall. The passages come from Alham Ahmed's compilation <em>The Gospel According to the Quran: Jesus and His Teachings</em> (Toronto: Noor Lal Foundation, 2021), which gathers 145 verses on Jesus and his teachings from thirteen chapters of the Qur'an, in 45 passages, each under its own heading. The translation is <em>The Clear Quran</em> by Dr. Mustafa Khattab, which the compiler acknowledges using with the permission of the Al-Furqaan Foundation.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Twenty-two of these passages stand inside the sections, under the compilation's headings: "Jesus Is a Messenger" and "Jesus and His Followers" where the disciples are called; "Do Not Say Trinity", "One God, Not Three" and "Jesus Is Not God" where the gospel reaches the question of his nature; "Jesus Prophesied the Coming of Muhammad" at the promise of the Counsellor; "Conspiracy Against Jesus" and "Jesus Was Neither Killed nor Crucified" at the arrest and the trial; "God's Favour upon Jesus" and "Jesus Denying Claim to Divinity" after the reports of the empty tomb. Every verse is numbered by chapter and verse in a green mark, so a reader always knows when the Qur'an is speaking and when the gospel text is. The full compilation, in the order of the Qur'an, is on <a href="/quran/passages/">The Qur'an on Jesus</a>, and the same ninety-three verses are grouped by theme on <a href="/quran/">Jesus in the Qur'an</a>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The notes</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>There are 235 notes, and they are Shafaat's own. They are numbered in one sequence through the whole narrative, so that note 127 means the same thing on every page, and each note is printed at the foot of the section in which its marker appears. A raised number in the text leads to the note; an arrow at the end of the note leads back. Where a note refers to another ("see note 19", "notes 49 and 50"), the reference is a link to that note on its own page. The notes do three things: they give the Qur'anic and New Testament passages behind each sentence of the text; they explain where and why Shafaat departed from a gospel source; and they set out the critical scholarship that bears on a passage, with the dates and manuscripts that matter. They are the apparatus of the gospel, and a reader who wants to know why a sentence reads as it does should start there.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The Gospel of Thomas</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>One body of material is this website's own addition. The <a href="/gospels/thomas/">Gospel of Thomas</a> is a collection of 114 sayings with no narrative: no birth, no miracles, no arrest, no crucifixion. Scholars date it between the late first century and the middle of the second, and about half its sayings have a parallel in Matthew, Mark or Luke. It was not available to the classical Muslim writers, and Shafaat did not use it. Every one of its sayings was tested against the measure below, and the result is recorded saying by saying.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Seventy sayings are placed in the gospel, under the heading "From the Gospel of Thomas" at the end of the section they belong to, each with its saying number, a colour mark and a note giving its canonical parallel and the Qur'anic verse it agrees with or the reason for its mark. Forty agree with the Qur'an as the measure and are marked green. Sixteen are beyond the measure, meaning that the Qur'an says nothing either way; they are included and marked blue. Fourteen are unclear, because the text is damaged or the sense is not settled; they are included and marked gold. The other forty-four are set aside: thirty-seven because they say what the Qur'an denies or deny what it affirms, and seven because they only repeat a saying already used. Each is listed with its reason on <a href="/gospels/thomas/set-aside/">the page of sayings set aside</a>. The saying numbers follow the standard edition; the summaries are this website's own, prepared from Thomas O. Lambdin's translation in <em>The Nag Hammadi Library in English</em> (Brill, 1988).</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>One rule of reading is worth stating. Thomas often says "the Father" where Matthew says "heaven" or Mark says "God". Where the canonical parallel makes the same point in other words, the saying is used and the note says so. A saying is set aside only when "Father" carries a claim of sonship or shared nature, which the Qur'an rejects (5:18; 19:35; 112:3). The Qur'an itself does not call God "Father", so this is a reading and not a settled rule, and it is applied in the open.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The early witnesses to the substitution</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Two further passages are woven in, at <a href="/gospel-according-to-islam/26/">section 26</a>, where the gospels have Simon of Cyrene carry the cross. Both are early Christian witnesses to what the Qur’an states in 4:157, that the crucifixion was made to appear otherwise to those who carried it out. The Gospel of Basilides, if it was a gospel, is lost; what Basilides taught in Alexandria in the early second century is known only from Irenaeus, who reported it around 180 CE in order to condemn it: that Simon was made to look like Jesus and Jesus like Simon, that Simon was crucified in his place, and that Jesus was not crucified but returned to the One who sent him. The Second Treatise of the Great Seth, a third-century Coptic text found at Nag Hammadi, has the risen Christ say that he did not die in reality but in appearance, that another drank the gall, another was struck with the reed, another, Simon, bore the cross, and another wore the crown of thorns. Tested against the measure, the substitution itself agrees with the Qur’an and with the reading of the classical commentators, and it is kept, marked green, in each case with a note naming the source and its character. What surrounds it is set aside, and the notes say exactly what: the laughter both texts attribute to Jesus, which is the Gnostic contempt for the body and not the Qur’an’s messenger of mercy; the Seth text’s denial that he had a real body; and its mockery of Adam, Abraham, Moses and the prophets, and of the God who says there is no other beside Him, which the Qur’an makes its first truth. The two texts are kept as witnesses, not as teaching. The rule for Thomas applies to them: what conflicts with the Qur’an is left out, and the reader is told.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The measure, applied</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Everything above answers to one rule, stated in full on <a href="/method/">the measure</a>. The Qur'an judges which gospel material is kept. Where gospel material agrees with the Qur'an, it is used. Where it contradicts the Qur'an, it is set aside, and the page says so. Where the Qur'an is silent, material is used only if mainstream critical scholarship treats it as early, and it is labelled as such. The Qur'an calls itself a guardian over the earlier scripture (<em>muhaymin</em>, 5:48), and that is the office it holds here. The rule is not a licence to keep what is congenial. A saying of Thomas that is pleasant but conflicts with the Qur'an is set aside, and the reason is printed; a gospel passage that is hard but agrees with the Qur'an is kept.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The colour marks make the rule visible. Green means the material agrees with the Qur'an. Blue means the Qur'an is silent and the material is kept on the strength of its attestation. Gold means the material is unclear and is kept with that warning. Red means conflict, and grey means repetition; material under those two marks appears only on the set-aside page, never in the gospel. A reader can therefore see at a glance, on any page, how much of what is in front of them the Qur'an confirms, how much it allows, and how much it leaves open.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What this gospel is not</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>It is not a translation of a lost book. No manuscript of the Injīl given to ʿĪsā survives, and this website does not claim to have found one. It is not a harmony of the four gospels, since a harmony keeps everything and this gospel sets things aside. It is not a historian's life of Jesus, which would weigh every source alike and would have no place for revelation. It is a gospel in the sense Shafaat gave the word: the story of Jesus told so as to bring out its meaning for faith and for history, written in the light of the revelation given to Muhammad. The Christian reader is asked to read it as he would read a book from a church other than his own, and to judge it by the sources it names rather than by the name on its cover.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
